Title: Do not look for Media Foundation where it cannot exist
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

Every process that initialises media on Windows XP or Server 2003 logged

  ERROR:media\base\win\mf_initializer.cc:46] Failed to start Media
  Foundation, accelerated media functionality may be disabled. If you're
  using Windows N, see https://support.microsoft.com/... for information on
  how to install the Media Feature Pack. Error: Could not load mf.dll: The
  specified module could not be found. (0x7E)

which is true and useless. Media Foundation was introduced in Windows Vista;
on anything older mf.dll is not missing, it never existed, and there is no
feature pack to install.

LoadMediaFoundationLibraries() now returns false up front on a pre-Vista
system, before trying to load anything. That is the value it returned there
already, after failing, so nothing downstream changes: InitializeMediaFoundation()
and PreSandboxMediaFoundationInitialization() report no Media Foundation and
their callers fall back to software decoding, as they did. The session
singleton is never constructed, since InitializeMediaFoundation() only reaches
it when the libraries loaded.

Vista is left alone. It has mf.dll, so the load is still attempted there and
any failure still reported.

diff --git a/media/base/win/mf_initializer.cc b/media/base/win/mf_initializer.cc
--- a/media/base/win/mf_initializer.cc
+++ b/media/base/win/mf_initializer.cc
@@ -17,6 +17,7 @@
 #include "base/threading/thread_restrictions.h"
 #include "base/win/delayload_helpers.h"
 #include "base/win/scoped_handle.h"
+#include "base/win/windows_version.h"
 #include "media/base/media_switches.h"
 #include "media/base/win/media_foundation_package_runtime_locator.h"
 
@@ -38,6 +39,16 @@
 // sandbox initialization or it will always fail.
 bool LoadMediaFoundationLibraries() {
   static const bool kDidLoadSucceed = []() {
+    // Media Foundation arrived with Windows Vista. Before that there is no
+    // mf.dll to look for, so say no without trying: the attempt can only fail,
+    // and it fails loudly -- an ERROR in every process that asks, advising the
+    // user to install a Media Feature Pack that does not exist for their
+    // system. Callers already treat false as "no accelerated media", and
+    // playback goes through the software decoders as it did anyway.
+    if (base::win::GetVersion() < base::win::Version::VISTA) {
+      return false;
+    }
+
     ScopedAllowBlockingForMediaFoundation allow_io_to_load_library;
     for (const wchar_t* mfdll : {L"mf.dll", L"mfplat.dll"}) {
       base::NativeLibraryLoadError error;
